WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.341 --> 00:00:04.508
(audience applauding)

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.930
- All right, well, welcome, everyone.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.960
We're really sorry Gina
Raimondo couldn't make it today,

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.980
but my sincere hope is
that she will manage

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.670
to keep all of us doing our
necessary work here (chuckles)

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.280
with her duties in Washington.
(audience chuckling)

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.570
So good afternoon.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:25.890
I'm Jeff Brock.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:27.330
I'm the Dean of the School of Engineering

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:28.950
and Applied Science,

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.270
and it gives me great pleasure

00:00:33.182 --> 00:00:36.600
to bring today the second of our series

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.660
in the "Dean's Invited Speaker Series."

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:44.280
These are a new series
that we've inaugurated

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.100
to try to bring members of our community,

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:51.570
the larger Yale community, who
are highly placed in industry

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:54.420
into conversation with all of you

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.180
and with the larger Yale community.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:00.330
So many of our alums have made their way

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:05.330
to the top of the industrial
world in one role or another.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:07.530
They have incredible stories.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:11.430
And our guest today
really implementizes that

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:14.793
probably more than any of our alums.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:20.580
So I'm really pleased to welcome C.C. Wei,

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.750
who joins us in part, in
addition to this grand event,

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:28.020
to receive the Wilbur
Cross Medal this evening

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.040
from the Graduate School
of Arts and Sciences.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.076
Let's give a round of applause for that.

00:01:33.076 --> 00:01:36.243
(audience applauding)

00:01:39.803 --> 00:01:43.413
So just a couple of words
about C.C.'s trajectory,

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.960
he received his graduate degree,
his PhD, here in the 1980s,

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.610
just before I arrived on campus.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.400
We just missed each other. (chuckles)

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:58.400
And he then went on to a
role as a research scientist

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:03.030
and working at Texas Instruments,
semiconductor corporation.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:04.560
Ultimately, found his way

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.730
to leading Taiwan Semiconductor
Manufacturing Company,

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:12.060
or TSMC, which has been in the news,

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.853
and which you no doubt have
heard of if you're here.

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:17.940
So I won't go through all of the twists

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:22.170
and turns of his career and the
other academic institutions,

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:24.090
which may deserve some measure of credit

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:29.090
for his arriving at this
sort of pinnacle in industry.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:34.400
But really, my goal is to
have just an easy conversation

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.870
with Dr. Wei here

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:40.140
and to hear about some
recent collaboration

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.500
that he has been involved
in with the United States

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:47.100
and how that reflects his worldview.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:50.250
And so we'll just engage
in about 25 minutes

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:51.930
or so of conversation,

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:55.290
and then I'm eager to open it
up to this wonderful crowd.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:57.120
And thank you, again, all for coming.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.443
Now let me welcome C.C. Wei.

00:02:59.443 --> 00:03:02.610
(audience applauding)

00:03:05.467 --> 00:03:06.300
- [C.C.] Thank you, Jeff.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:07.133
- Okay.

00:03:09.749 --> 00:03:11.670
Now maybe you'll turn down
the volume for a minute,

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:13.263
so we can open our Diet Cokes.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.403
Exactly. (chuckles)

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.470
All right, so I have the honor

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:36.510
and privilege of opening it up here today,

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:41.510
but I guess I'm just curious
how you came to Yale,

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.200
how you saw Yale as a
direction you wanted to pursue,

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.510
and what you think it gave you

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:50.643
in terms of your trajectory forward.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.420
- That's a good question.

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:56.010
- You are wearing a Yale tie,

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:59.400
so it must have meant
something to you. (laughs)

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.490
- Well, starting with this tie,

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:02.880
(audience laughing)

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:07.695
this is a only chance I
can wear a tie with proud.

00:04:07.695 --> 00:04:09.140
I mean, that's a...
(audience laughing)

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:12.180
If I wear this one in my company,

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.767
a lot of people will protest.

00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:15.681
(audience laughing)

00:04:15.681 --> 00:04:17.640
Because they graduate
from the different school.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.207
They think they are the best.

00:04:20.207 --> 00:04:21.040
(audience laughing)

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.369
Anyway, here is the best.

00:04:23.369 --> 00:04:24.656
(audience laughing)

00:04:24.656 --> 00:04:27.823
(audience applauding)

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:32.043
Look, why I join Yale?

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:34.410
It's interesting.

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:36.390
This is because of it's a name brand.

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:38.793
You know that what is called a name brand?

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:44.250
The Harvard, Yale, and Stanford, MIT,

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.260
those are the name brand.

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:47.370
So when I receive...

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:52.370
I apply many schools when I
want to have a graduate study.

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:56.250
And one day, I received the admission,

00:04:56.250 --> 00:05:00.900
and I still waiting for other
school to give me the reply,

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.990
but then my family, my brother especially,

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:08.370
he say, "Hey, young brother, this is Yale.

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:11.310
Is that the same as in the newspaper?

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:12.660
Is a Harvard, Yale?"

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:13.920
I say, "Yes."

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:16.860
He say, "Then what are you waiting for?

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:18.270
Just go over there."
(audience laughing)

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:23.160
I mean, I say, "Well, I'm still
waiting for Carnegie Mellon

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:24.660
or those kind of a school."

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:26.602
He say, "What the melon?

00:05:26.602 --> 00:05:28.877
Melon is a fruit.

00:05:28.877 --> 00:05:29.710
(audience laughing)

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:30.627
Don't go over there."

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.560
So, well, I accepted Yale.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:38.130
But joke aside, actually, Professor Ma

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:42.300
is one of the reason that
I decide to join Yale.

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:47.300
And because of I have my
school professor, Dr. C.Y. Wu,

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:54.060
he's a classmate of Professor Ma,

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:59.060
and I think that Professor
Wu is a nice professor,

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:04.088
then Professor Ma must be as good as him.

00:06:04.088 --> 00:06:05.970
In fact, he's better.

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:07.530
He's the best.
(audience laughing)

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:10.920
And I learned a lot from Professor Ma.

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:15.810
And today, Professor Ma's wife is here

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:18.390
and I'm grateful to them.

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:19.792
Pin-Fang, yes.

00:06:19.792 --> 00:06:22.959
(audience applauding)

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:30.720
And then to tell you truth,
when I was studying Yale,

00:06:34.344 --> 00:06:38.353
it's a endless work, a lot of pressure,

00:06:38.353 --> 00:06:40.590
and a long working hour.

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:43.767
So I thought, I say,
"This is hell of my life.

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:47.264
I need to get out as quickly as possible."

00:06:47.264 --> 00:06:48.510
(audience laughing)

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:52.710
And I tried, but I failed, of course.

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:56.550
You still have to stay five
years and something like that.

00:06:56.550 --> 00:07:01.410
And in between, I talked
to Professor Barker,

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:03.516
we are in the same group.

00:07:03.516 --> 00:07:08.516
I say, "Professor Barker,
how can I get out quickly?"

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:10.950
Then he look at me, he say, "You don't.

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:12.570
You don't get out.

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:15.030
Have you ever enjoy Yale?"

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:17.010
I say, "How to enjoy Yale?"

00:07:17.010 --> 00:07:19.530
Yeah.
(audience laughing)

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:22.080
In my life, it's the Helen Hadley Hall,

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:26.910
the tunnel to Becton Center,
the library in Becton Center,

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:28.860
and never-ending.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:32.040
So he look at me, he say,
"Have you ever listened

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:35.604
to any speech in the law school?"

00:07:35.604 --> 00:07:37.269
I say, "What the hell?

00:07:37.269 --> 00:07:39.000
Why I need to...
(audience laughing)

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:40.410
I want to graduate.

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:41.250
That's all, right?"

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:42.510
I mean, that's a...

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:45.360
So he did not give me the right answer.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.640
So I be patient.

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:51.821
But finally, finally, what I got from Yale

00:07:51.821 --> 00:07:54.660
is I got a PhD degree,

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:59.660
but I also very proud
to meet one young lady

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:04.503
that I fell in love with, and
then finally become my wife.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:07.019
My wife is here, Jessica.

00:08:07.019 --> 00:08:10.186
(audience applauding)

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:18.240
And then with her, I have
three lovely kids, okay?

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.719
One of them is with me, Terry.

00:08:21.719 --> 00:08:25.080
(audience applauding)

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:27.613
Okay, I tell the truth.

00:08:27.613 --> 00:08:29.070
I got what I want.

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:31.680
I have a degree, I have my wife,

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:34.620
and then the life goes on, right?

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:39.620
And I thought that would
be a rosy picture ahead.

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:44.010
But actually, it's not.

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:47.460
If you work on the semiconductor industry,

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:48.873
you have to work hard.

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:53.793
It's by nature because
of this semiconductor

00:08:53.793 --> 00:08:55.950
is a very important industry.

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:57.420
Finally, we knew that.

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:01.011
But 40 years ago, it's not.

00:09:01.011 --> 00:09:04.770
40 years ago, EE is not so,

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:07.920
let me say that, is not so popular.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:10.170
Now today, is more important.

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:13.102
Okay, I got all my story in here.

00:09:13.102 --> 00:09:14.970
(Jeff chuckles)
(audience laughing)

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:19.140
- So I was telling C.C. earlier
that semiconductors are hot.

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:21.513
Look at the presence here today.

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:25.500
So what would you say

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:29.880
about kind of the American
culture resonates with you

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:34.880
in terms of developing
creativity, productivity?

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:40.490
Why might you be inclined to
work with the US government

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:42.873
to develop this partnership?

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:45.990
- That's a good question.

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:48.570
Actually, there's a lot of rumor says

00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:53.370
that TSMC had been
pressured by US government

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:56.193
to come to US to build a fab.

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:01.650
To tell the truth, it's
not really that story.

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:06.540
In Taiwan, we start to
see the limitation of...

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:11.940
The number one limitation
that in Taiwan I have

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:14.220
is talented people.

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:18.420
The limitation of amount of talent people.

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:23.073
And to tell the truth, US is a country

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:28.623
that with the most innovative,
talented people here,

00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:31.473
like you guy are, okay?

00:10:32.758 --> 00:10:37.758
Very innovative, very kind
of wild dream-driven people.

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:47.990
And especially for the Ivy League student,

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:51.930
they're always being
trained to be the leader.

00:10:51.930 --> 00:10:56.460
So you guys don't think something small.

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:59.850
You will be the leader in your industry,

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:02.460
in your career one day.

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:05.190
And so now talk about why TSMC want

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:07.710
to build a fab in the US.

00:11:07.710 --> 00:11:11.370
Simple, because of US
where have a huge amount

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:14.370
of talent people that I can look for,

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:18.420
and we can have some
select of the talent people

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:21.993
to join TSMC in US.

00:11:23.007 --> 00:11:26.100
At the same time, the US also
think about the government,

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:29.640
also think about semiconductor
is very important.

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:33.757
It indeed is very important
because this semiconductor

00:11:35.190 --> 00:11:38.245
is a center of every technology.

00:11:38.245 --> 00:11:39.640
You name it.

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:41.100
In the future, you name it.

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:46.100
You want to have more
advance in medical care?

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:48.390
You need a semiconductor.

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:50.643
You want to have autonomous driving?

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:53.580
You need a semiconductor.

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:58.580
You want to have a AI
application in every industry?

00:11:58.590 --> 00:12:00.600
You need semiconductor.

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:03.600
So US government suddenly realize that.

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:07.500
I say suddenly because
they never talk with TSMC.

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:09.408
Now, they talk very often.

00:12:09.408 --> 00:12:11.850
(audience laughing)

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:16.850
So actually, I expect
to have a same occasion

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:20.433
that Secretary Raimondo here.

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:24.993
But anyway, so finally,

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:29.400
I mean, why we choose US?

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:31.314
It's not we choose US.

00:12:31.314 --> 00:12:33.300
This is a natural choice

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:37.110
because you need the talent
of people here, okay?

00:12:37.110 --> 00:12:40.770
And by the way, US
government also promised

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:45.770
to help TSMC to build the fab in Arizona.

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:50.730
So we choose it, we like it,
and we have some problem.

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:53.310
No doubt, you just read the newspaper.

00:12:53.310 --> 00:12:56.970
But then we will overcome
all those kind of thing.

00:12:56.970 --> 00:13:01.320
We took it as a kind of a learning cycle.

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:04.980
And then we're going to
build more fab in the US.

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:07.535
And welcome you guys to join, by the way.

00:13:07.535 --> 00:13:09.780
(Jeff chuckles)
(audience laughing)

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:14.350
- So you mentioned the
characteristics of the undergraduates

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:19.023
and graduate students that we
train in terms of leadership.

00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:24.660
What would you see as challenges
that universities face now

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:26.640
in terms of technical training?

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:29.310
What kinds of techniques
should we be emphasizing

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:30.513
in terms of curriculum?

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:33.870
- Technical?

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:34.920
That's interesting.

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:38.068
I never touched that area
for more than 10 years.

00:13:38.068 --> 00:13:40.711
(audience laughing)

00:13:40.711 --> 00:13:44.486
Nowadays, I use my lip service.

00:13:44.486 --> 00:13:45.540
(audience laughing)

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:46.571
- Same here, by the way.

00:13:46.571 --> 00:13:48.210
(all laughing)

00:13:48.210 --> 00:13:49.534
- That's why we sit here.

00:13:49.534 --> 00:13:50.880
- Yeah. (laughs)
(audience laughing)

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:53.280
- Actually, my wife joke with me,

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:55.980
say that if I cannot speak it out,

00:13:55.980 --> 00:14:00.980
I can ask my kids to help
me to deliver the speech.

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:08.388
In undergraduate...

00:14:08.388 --> 00:14:09.840
Let me use one joke.

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:12.570
One time I talked with Professor Barker.

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:16.410
So Professor Barker say
that, as a professor,

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:20.070
he has to treat the
undergraduate student very well

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:23.883
because most of them will be
the leader in the industry,

00:14:24.810 --> 00:14:28.383
for the good student, when
you have a good track record.

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:34.140
When the student have a good track record,

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:35.670
they will become professor,

00:14:35.670 --> 00:14:38.130
so they will become his colleagues.

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:40.849
So deal with them politely.

00:14:40.849 --> 00:14:41.682
(audience laughing)

00:14:41.682 --> 00:14:46.220
When the student with a
bad record, the bad score,

00:14:47.382 --> 00:14:49.645
they will be the industry leader.

00:14:49.645 --> 00:14:50.940
(audience laughing)

00:14:50.940 --> 00:14:53.520
They will donate money to the school.

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:56.038
So be nice to them, also.

00:14:56.038 --> 00:14:57.540
(audience laughing)

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:00.210
For those graduate student, ignore them

00:15:00.210 --> 00:15:03.361
because of they are useless sometimes.

00:15:03.361 --> 00:15:05.430
(audience laughing)

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:07.353
Okay, now joke aside,

00:15:08.970 --> 00:15:13.970
I would think the best Ivy
League, so the school, actually,

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:20.430
to train the future leader,
that's very important.

00:15:20.430 --> 00:15:24.360
I'm not very good at the
technical, to be frank with you.

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:26.223
I'm a mediocre student.

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:31.391
When Professor Ma give me
the score, it's mediocre.

00:15:31.391 --> 00:15:32.224
(Jeff chuckles)

00:15:32.224 --> 00:15:36.013
So I'm the one that
graduated, but this school,

00:15:38.310 --> 00:15:43.310
Yale, this school, will
train the future leader.

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:47.010
You look at how many
of US president we have

00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:48.330
from this school.

00:15:48.330 --> 00:15:50.839
We're the best in the law school, right?

00:15:50.839 --> 00:15:52.530
(Jeff chuckles)

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:54.030
Engineering, I don't know.

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:55.424
I mean, that's-
- We're getting,

00:15:55.424 --> 00:15:57.870
we're getting, right?
(audience laughing)

00:15:57.870 --> 00:15:59.010
- Yeah.

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:02.133
But then we will be the
leaders in the industry.

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:04.103
Look at me.

00:16:04.103 --> 00:16:06.630
I don't need to have a very good score,

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:09.286
but I still can be the leader, yeah.

00:16:09.286 --> 00:16:10.119
(audience laughing)

00:16:10.119 --> 00:16:12.240
Don't worry, you will get it.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:14.995
By the way, the trick is
that you had to live longer.

00:16:14.995 --> 00:16:15.975
- Right, yeah.

00:16:15.975 --> 00:16:17.780
(audience laughing)

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:18.873
So stay healthy.

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:22.080
Okay, so you've teed up my next question,

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:25.620
which is, at what point
when you were working

00:16:25.620 --> 00:16:29.220
through your career did
you see opportunities

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:30.150
for leadership?

00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:32.370
What made you choose to
head in that direction

00:16:32.370 --> 00:16:35.700
as opposed to research and engineering?

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:37.260
- Well, the answer is very simple.

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:39.081
I'm not good at research.

00:16:39.081 --> 00:16:40.680
(audience laughing)

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:43.653
So by nature, you have to
choose another route, right?

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:49.640
To be frank with you, in my
career, we had to be humble.

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:56.940
We had to be humble
during my whole career.

00:16:56.940 --> 00:16:59.010
So I know a lot of talented people.

00:16:59.010 --> 00:17:03.033
They can do much better work
than me in terms of technical.

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:08.910
But I'm not too bad
either, by the way, okay?

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:13.910
I have a few papers and
about 30 to 40 patent,

00:17:17.725 --> 00:17:22.710
30 to 40 papers and speeches.

00:17:22.710 --> 00:17:26.220
But then I'm not the very best of it.

00:17:26.220 --> 00:17:28.760
However, during your career,

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:35.187
you continue to be humble, humility,

00:17:37.470 --> 00:17:40.203
and you had to be,

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:46.980
love what you have doing with your work.

00:17:46.980 --> 00:17:49.110
That's a trick. That's a trick.

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:50.343
You've got to be happy.

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:55.170
Only when you are happy,
you can work hard.

00:17:55.170 --> 00:17:57.150
But how to become happy,

00:17:57.150 --> 00:18:01.080
well, how you are going to love your work,

00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:06.080
you have to find the
pleasure inside your work.

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:07.530
That's a trick.

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:10.743
If you cannot find the
pleasure, don't do it.

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:15.600
Change to another company
or change your boss.

00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:17.355
I don't know, ah, usually.

00:18:17.355 --> 00:18:19.170
(audience laughing)

00:18:19.170 --> 00:18:23.850
But in reality, you cannot
change your boss, right?

00:18:23.850 --> 00:18:27.480
So you try to find some
pleasure with your work,

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:30.963
and then you are become
happy, and then you work hard.

00:18:32.670 --> 00:18:36.600
I always believe that everybody's
a wisdom almost equal.

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:39.570
You are not much smarter
than other people,

00:18:39.570 --> 00:18:42.480
but working hard, enjoy it,

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:45.210
and then you can work all the way through.

00:18:45.210 --> 00:18:50.190
And by nature, you are become
the leader of your group

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:52.800
and then become the
leader of a bigger group

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:56.943
and become the leader of a
company, like me right now.

00:18:59.370 --> 00:19:03.047
To be frank with you, I
am lucky all the time,

00:19:03.047 --> 00:19:04.260
(audience chuckling)

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:05.790
whether you believe you or not.

00:19:05.790 --> 00:19:06.623
- Mm-hmm.

00:19:08.238 --> 00:19:13.238
(chuckles) So your advisor, T.P. Ma,

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:18.830
was one of these larger-than-life
sort of warm personas,

00:19:19.170 --> 00:19:21.630
and we miss him dearly.

00:19:21.630 --> 00:19:24.720
Probably there were nevertheless
moments when you had doubts

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:26.880
about your chosen direction,

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:29.160
as we all do as graduate students.

00:19:29.160 --> 00:19:32.373
What were the things that kept you going?

00:19:33.330 --> 00:19:36.690
What can you offer this
group in terms of motivation

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:38.613
for the hard moments?

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:41.670
- Yeah, so what motivation?

00:19:41.670 --> 00:19:45.510
'Cause when I was a graduate student,

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:47.430
the life is tough, right?

00:19:47.430 --> 00:19:50.190
I mean, I have to work for a long hour

00:19:50.190 --> 00:19:52.350
all the way to the night.

00:19:52.350 --> 00:19:56.010
So the motivation is to
get out of this school

00:19:56.010 --> 00:19:57.204
as quickly as possible.

00:19:57.204 --> 00:19:59.127
(audience laughing)

00:19:59.127 --> 00:20:01.849
And then I will have a better life, right?

00:20:01.849 --> 00:20:03.420
- [Jeff] Yeah, yeah.

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:07.470
- The same motivation that
when I was working in TI.

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:10.670
I'm an engineer, so I
say, "What I am doing?

00:20:12.522 --> 00:20:14.370
I still work day and night."

00:20:14.370 --> 00:20:17.070
So I want to be promoted to manager

00:20:17.070 --> 00:20:18.929
so I will have a better life.

00:20:18.929 --> 00:20:19.762
(Jeff chuckles)
(audience chuckling)

00:20:19.762 --> 00:20:22.680
So once I get the managerial job,

00:20:22.680 --> 00:20:27.680
I say, "Oh, boy, I got to be
a VP, so then I can relax."

00:20:28.272 --> 00:20:29.820
(audience chuckling)

00:20:29.820 --> 00:20:34.820
So I was a VP in TSMC.

00:20:35.490 --> 00:20:36.600
Then I found out,

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:41.600
God, boy, I work for a very
top boss, Dr. Morris Chang.

00:20:42.900 --> 00:20:45.630
So I say, "Oh, boy, I mean, that's a...

00:20:45.630 --> 00:20:48.450
I got to be a CEO to get rid

00:20:48.450 --> 00:20:52.470
of this nightmare."
(audience laughing)

00:20:52.470 --> 00:20:54.242
And then I start to regret

00:20:54.242 --> 00:20:57.266
that the nightmare never disappear.

00:20:57.266 --> 00:20:58.110
(audience laughing)

00:20:58.110 --> 00:20:59.310
So motivation?

00:20:59.310 --> 00:21:04.147
Motivation is usually you
think the future is better, but

00:21:06.492 --> 00:21:07.800
(audience laughing)

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:09.167
it's not really.

00:21:09.167 --> 00:21:10.950
(audience laughing)

00:21:10.950 --> 00:21:14.013
So just give you a good
advice, okay, guys?

00:21:15.030 --> 00:21:19.170
You realize that in your
life you always have a dream

00:21:19.170 --> 00:21:22.811
and the dream come true, but
not the same dream as you have.

00:21:22.811 --> 00:21:25.050
(Jeff chuckles)

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:27.960
- All right, well, I
think that's what I wanted

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:31.140
to ask our friend, but I'm
sure you have many questions

00:21:31.140 --> 00:21:34.140
you might want to pose to C.C.

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:35.943
I don't know if there are any hands?

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:38.880
Anyone wanna raise a question?

00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:40.200
Yeah, over here.

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:41.600
Thank you, Elizabeth.

00:21:44.010 --> 00:21:47.970
- The vast majority of US military planes

00:21:47.970 --> 00:21:50.490
have Taiwanese chips in them.

00:21:50.490 --> 00:21:53.880
And the semiconductors
were made in your company.

00:21:53.880 --> 00:21:58.880
And I'm just wondering where
you think the supply lines

00:22:00.180 --> 00:22:04.983
for these chips will
evolve in the coming years?

00:22:07.620 --> 00:22:10.767
Thank you.

00:22:10.767 --> 00:22:12.597
- You can either
(audience laughing)

00:22:12.597 --> 00:22:15.030
(laughs) agree or decline

00:22:15.030 --> 00:22:16.710
to answer this.
(C.C. laughs)

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:19.440
- That's a interesting question

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:24.000
that I don't have a perfect answer.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:26.170
I mean, that you are talking about

00:22:28.110 --> 00:22:30.720
like the US government official talking

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:34.650
to me that...
(audience laughing)

00:22:34.650 --> 00:22:39.030
This is related to the national security

00:22:39.030 --> 00:22:44.030
or related to the resilience
of the supply to the US.

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:49.563
To be frank with you, in my point of view,

00:22:51.390 --> 00:22:54.513
the semiconductor industry is globalized.

00:22:55.590 --> 00:22:59.673
Every country doing
what he can do the best.

00:23:01.230 --> 00:23:06.230
But among them, the biggest,
let me say not the biggest,

00:23:07.950 --> 00:23:12.950
the strongest country in
semiconductor industry is the USA.

00:23:15.810 --> 00:23:16.643
Okay.

00:23:16.643 --> 00:23:21.600
You guys don't get
misled by the newspaper,

00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:26.600
by all the rumors say
that, "Oh, manufacturing?

00:23:27.390 --> 00:23:32.390
Taiwan is very good," and blah blah blah.

00:23:32.569 --> 00:23:33.402
(Jeff chuckles)

00:23:33.402 --> 00:23:38.030
The strongest country in
semiconductor industry is USA.

00:23:41.460 --> 00:23:46.460
Now the US is kind of
putting manufacturing aside

00:23:50.130 --> 00:23:51.870
for a while,

00:23:51.870 --> 00:23:56.870
but if US decide to pick
it up quickly, they will.

00:23:57.240 --> 00:24:01.230
And why I say the strongest country

00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:05.760
in semiconductor industry is
because of all the process,

00:24:05.760 --> 00:24:07.740
all the technology development.

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:09.963
You need the very advanced tools.

00:24:11.130 --> 00:24:14.220
And all the advanced tool originated,

00:24:14.220 --> 00:24:16.863
all being manufactured in the US.

00:24:17.730 --> 00:24:20.460
That's why it become the strongest.

00:24:20.460 --> 00:24:24.153
I using the photolithography
as one of the example.

00:24:25.050 --> 00:24:29.113
Today, the most advanced
photolithography tool is a UV.

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:33.243
Is anybody know what's called UV?

00:24:35.820 --> 00:24:36.653
That's all?

00:24:36.653 --> 00:24:38.820
And all other people didn't know?

00:24:38.820 --> 00:24:41.790
Okay, now, let's say anybody

00:24:41.790 --> 00:24:46.790
who did not understand UV stands
for, except my wife, okay.

00:24:48.301 --> 00:24:49.420
(audience laughing)

00:24:49.420 --> 00:24:50.550
Okay, good.

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:55.073
I mean, that's kind of a
very small wavelength tools.

00:24:56.370 --> 00:25:01.370
And the central part
of the UV is in the US,

00:25:02.010 --> 00:25:03.660
the light source.

00:25:03.660 --> 00:25:06.690
The light source to generate such kind

00:25:06.690 --> 00:25:11.460
of a very short
wavelengths is from the US.

00:25:11.460 --> 00:25:12.900
No doubt about it.

00:25:12.900 --> 00:25:16.380
And using TSMC as an example,

00:25:16.380 --> 00:25:19.710
I use a lot of Applied
Materials as a tools.

00:25:19.710 --> 00:25:24.710
I use a lot of Lam Research
as a tool, and KLA-Tencor,

00:25:27.076 --> 00:25:31.193
and a lot of software from Mentor Graphic,

00:25:32.820 --> 00:25:35.540
or now it become Siemens, or...

00:25:37.500 --> 00:25:39.090
Okay, I forgot their name.

00:25:39.090 --> 00:25:40.530
I'm sorry for that.

00:25:40.530 --> 00:25:41.880
But Cadence,
(Jeff chuckles)

00:25:41.880 --> 00:25:46.880
and then Synopsys, and those
guys, those are from US.

00:25:48.750 --> 00:25:52.500
Without them, you cannot develop

00:25:52.500 --> 00:25:55.500
or you cannot design any product.

00:25:55.500 --> 00:26:00.500
So part of the reason I
can answer Jeff's question,

00:26:01.627 --> 00:26:05.280
"Why choose US as a partner?"

00:26:05.280 --> 00:26:08.550
Of course, this is the best country

00:26:08.550 --> 00:26:10.740
for the semiconducting industry.

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:12.540
What else I can choose?

00:26:12.540 --> 00:26:16.530
So if I have a choice,

00:26:16.530 --> 00:26:20.643
US is the number one place I will choose.

00:26:21.600 --> 00:26:26.600
Produce enough chips
inside US takes time, okay?

00:26:27.600 --> 00:26:29.247
So be patient.

00:26:29.247 --> 00:26:30.570
(audience laughing)

00:26:30.570 --> 00:26:32.310
That's the same answer I answer

00:26:32.310 --> 00:26:34.934
that US government officials.

00:26:34.934 --> 00:26:36.390
(audience laughing)

00:26:36.390 --> 00:26:37.320
Be patient.

00:26:37.320 --> 00:26:38.193
- Be patient.

00:26:39.150 --> 00:26:40.687
Okay, there's another one over here.

00:26:40.687 --> 00:26:41.937
- [Microphone Runner] Sorry.

00:26:41.937 --> 00:26:43.190
- Hi, I'm Frank.

00:26:43.190 --> 00:26:47.340
So my question is, even though US is one

00:26:47.340 --> 00:26:49.230
of the more higher-tech
countries in the world,

00:26:49.230 --> 00:26:53.460
what made Taiwan such a
fertile ecosystem for TSMC

00:26:53.460 --> 00:26:56.403
to develop there versus
being in the US originally?

00:26:58.290 --> 00:27:01.470
- Oh, you're talking about the ecosystem

00:27:01.470 --> 00:27:04.260
for the whole manufacturing
of a semiconductor?

00:27:04.260 --> 00:27:05.093
- Sure.

00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:08.659
- The same answer, be patient.

00:27:08.659 --> 00:27:09.630
(audience laughing)

00:27:09.630 --> 00:27:12.000
It takes time to build up, right?

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:13.290
I mean that's a...

00:27:13.290 --> 00:27:16.473
TSMC has been in Taiwan for 30 years.

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:22.400
The whole ecosystem, including
to set up a building,

00:27:23.580 --> 00:27:28.580
to set up a fab, the construction
worker, to be specific,

00:27:30.180 --> 00:27:33.810
and also, the facility
worker and everybody,

00:27:33.810 --> 00:27:37.380
and also, the chemical supply,
the raw material supply,

00:27:37.380 --> 00:27:42.360
it takes Taiwan for more
than 30 years to be mature.

00:27:42.360 --> 00:27:43.940
And so I would expect...

00:27:44.940 --> 00:27:47.403
I don't think the US need 30 years.

00:27:48.570 --> 00:27:50.670
US can catch up quickly

00:27:50.670 --> 00:27:54.360
because of this is the
best place in the world

00:27:54.360 --> 00:27:58.020
that you have a lot of talented people.

00:27:58.020 --> 00:28:03.020
And all the key is talented people.

00:28:03.030 --> 00:28:05.640
And so when US decide to focus

00:28:05.640 --> 00:28:08.133
on the semiconductor manufacturing,

00:28:09.307 --> 00:28:13.677
it will be catching up
quickly, but it takes time.

00:28:15.450 --> 00:28:16.283
It takes time.

00:28:16.283 --> 00:28:18.240
Not 30 years, of course.

00:28:18.240 --> 00:28:19.593
Much shorter than that.

00:28:23.240 --> 00:28:25.140
- (chuckles) All right, next question?

00:28:27.409 --> 00:28:28.680
- [Audience Member 1] First, I
wanna say thank you very much

00:28:28.680 --> 00:28:29.513
for coming.

00:28:29.513 --> 00:28:31.640
It's really a privilege to
be able to listen to you.

00:28:32.550 --> 00:28:36.330
I also ask, so since
you made the transition

00:28:36.330 --> 00:28:40.850
from being a engineer to a
manager, how did you know...

00:28:42.270 --> 00:28:43.560
Do you have any advice for being able

00:28:43.560 --> 00:28:45.840
to make that transition
from managing circuits

00:28:45.840 --> 00:28:46.740
to managing people?

00:28:46.740 --> 00:28:48.683
And do you have any
advice in regard to that?

00:28:50.430 --> 00:28:54.153
- Tell the truth, managing
people is very tough.

00:28:55.740 --> 00:28:58.500
You might want to read a book

00:28:58.500 --> 00:29:00.903
that's being written by President Salovey.

00:29:03.210 --> 00:29:08.210
He's a guy invented the EQ
and how to manage the people.

00:29:08.910 --> 00:29:10.759
Please read his book.

00:29:10.759 --> 00:29:12.240
(audience chuckling)

00:29:12.240 --> 00:29:15.400
I regret a lot that I
did not read his book

00:29:17.072 --> 00:29:20.373
and being promoted to a manager's job.

00:29:21.360 --> 00:29:25.192
I like to manage the machine
because they don't protest.

00:29:25.192 --> 00:29:29.010
(audience laughing)

00:29:29.010 --> 00:29:34.010
Managing the people are very
tough because of, for example,

00:29:34.230 --> 00:29:38.850
if they are not, have a
quarrel with his wife,

00:29:38.850 --> 00:29:42.150
then his temper become very bad.

00:29:42.150 --> 00:29:43.140
How did I know?

00:29:43.140 --> 00:29:47.100
I mean that yesterday,
he's my nice, good friend,

00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:49.830
and today, he show his face on me.

00:29:49.830 --> 00:29:52.320
So I don't understand that,

00:29:52.320 --> 00:29:55.470
so I give him a hard time just so...

00:29:55.470 --> 00:29:59.280
Usually, you change from a technical role

00:29:59.280 --> 00:30:01.830
to become managerial's role,

00:30:01.830 --> 00:30:06.693
you had to pay more attention
to people's daily life,

00:30:07.950 --> 00:30:12.950
which is very dynamic, and
so it's a very tough job.

00:30:13.800 --> 00:30:18.800
And so if you are very good
manager, I always ask my guy,

00:30:19.537 --> 00:30:23.100
"If you want to be
promoted to be a manager,

00:30:23.100 --> 00:30:25.898
you got to get married.

00:30:25.898 --> 00:30:27.810
(audience laughing)

00:30:27.810 --> 00:30:30.270
You know how to manage your family,

00:30:30.270 --> 00:30:33.025
then you are become a good manager."

00:30:33.025 --> 00:30:35.100
(audience chuckling)

00:30:35.100 --> 00:30:35.970
No joke.

00:30:35.970 --> 00:30:38.730
I tell them that, "If you are not married,

00:30:38.730 --> 00:30:40.898
I will not promote you to the manager."

00:30:40.898 --> 00:30:42.984
(audience laughing)

00:30:42.984 --> 00:30:44.790
(Jeff chuckles)

00:30:44.790 --> 00:30:45.623
- Over here.

00:30:49.537 --> 00:30:52.470
- [Audience Member 2] If and
how do you see TSMC essential

00:30:52.470 --> 00:30:57.177
in countering Chinese rising
global technological influence

00:30:57.177 --> 00:30:58.983
and semiconductor industry?

00:31:02.246 --> 00:31:04.830
- The question is, from my point of view,

00:31:04.830 --> 00:31:08.283
to look at how China?

00:31:09.267 --> 00:31:10.200
- [Audience Member 2] Yeah,

00:31:10.200 --> 00:31:12.120
countering China's semiconductor industry

00:31:12.120 --> 00:31:15.701
and your global technological influence.

00:31:15.701 --> 00:31:16.740
(Jeff chuckles)

00:31:16.740 --> 00:31:20.387
- I don't make a public
comment on China, by the way.

00:31:20.387 --> 00:31:21.945
(audience laughing)

00:31:21.945 --> 00:31:25.112
(audience applauding)

00:31:28.231 --> 00:31:29.850
To be frank with you guys,

00:31:29.850 --> 00:31:33.790
I was not allowed to give a speech

00:31:35.220 --> 00:31:37.950
